REGIONAL REGULATION OF PEKALONGAN CITY

NUMBER 5 OF 2015

CONCERNING

IMPLEMENTATION OF PROTECTION FOR VICTIMS OF GENDER-BASED VIOLENCE AND CHILDREN

BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD

MAYOR OF PEKALONGAN,

Considering:

a. that all forms of violence, especially gender-based violence and violence against children are violations of human rights and crimes against human dignity as well as forms of discrimination;
b. that victims of gender-based violence and violence against children must receive protection, both from the Regional Government, related agencies and/or the community, so that the community is protected and free from violence and/or threats of violence in the household and community;
c. that based on Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection, as amended by Law Number 35 of 2014 concerning Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection and Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence, the Regional Government, related agencies, and/or the community are obligated to undertake prevention, protection, and recovery efforts for victims of gender-based violence and violence against children;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b, and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning the Implementation of Protection for Victims of Gender-Based Violence and Children;

In View of:

1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 16 of 1950 concerning the Establishment of Large City Regions Within the Province of East Java, Central Java, West Java and the Special Region of Yogyakarta, as amended by Law Number 13 of 1954 concerning the Merger of Laws Number 16 and 17 of 1950 concerning the Establishment of Large Cities and Small Cities in Java (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1954 Number 40, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 551);
3. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235), as amended by Law Number 35 of 2014 concerning Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 297, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5606);
4. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
5. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587), as last amended by Law Number 9 of 2015 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 58 Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679);
6. Government Regulation Number 21 of 1988 concerning the Change of Regional Boundaries of the Second Level Municipality of Pekalongan, Second Level Regency of Pekalongan and Second Level Regency of Batang (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1988 Number 42, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3381);
7. Regional Regulation of Pekalongan City Number 11 of 2012 concerning the Implementation of Child Protection (Regional Gazette of Pekalongan City of 2011 Number 11);

With Mutual Approval

REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF PEKALONGAN CITY

and

MAYOR OF PEKALONGAN

HAS DECIDED:

To Establish:

REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING THE IMPLEMENTATION OF PROTECTION FOR VICTIMS OF GENDER-BASED VIOLENCE AND CHILDREN.

Article 2

Protection of victims of gender-based violence and children is based on Pancasila and grounded in the 1945 Constitution, as well as basic principles that include:
a. non-discrimination;
b. best interests of victims;
c. justice and gender equality;
d. victim protection;
e. survival, and development of children;
f. respect for the opinions of children;
g. transparency;
h. integration;
i. empowering; and
j. confidentiality of victims.

Article 3

1. The objectives of implementing protection for victims of gender-based violence and children are:
a. to prevent all forms of gender-based violence and violence against children, occurring within the scope of the household and/or community;
b. to provide protection;
c. to provide legal assistance;
d. to seek recovery and social reintegration; and/or
e. to increase community participation.
2. The objectives of implementing protection for victims of gender-based violence and children as referred to in paragraph (1), include aspects of:
a. prevention;
b. service and assistance;
c. reunification; and
d. empowerment.

CHAPTER III

RIGHTS OF VICTIMS

Article 4

Every victim of gender-based violence and child victims of violence have the right to receive:
a. protection for the security of themselves, their families, and their property, and freedom from threats related to information that will be, is being, or has been given;
b. participation in the process of selecting and determining the form of protection and security support;
c. information regarding case developments and court decisions;
d. services that are fast, appropriate, comfortable, and according to needs;
e. recovery and social reintegration;
f. legal assistance, psychological, spiritual guidance, economic, social and interpreter.

Article 5

Child victims of acts of violence, in addition to obtaining the rights as referred to in Article 4, also obtain special rights, as follows:
a. right to respect;
b. right to basic services;
c. right to equal protection;
d. right to be free from various stigma; and
e. right to obtain freedom;
f. full right to survival, growth and development.

Article 7

1. The obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in Article 6 letter a, include:
a. implementing protection policies against gender-based violence and children established by the government;
b. establishing policies, programs, and activities for protection against gender-based violence and children;
c. conducting cooperation in the implementation of protection for women and children from acts of violence;
d. providing support for facilities and infrastructure for the implementation of protection against gender-based violence and children;
e. allocating budget for the implementation of protection against gender-based violence and children in accordance with regional financial capacity; and
f. fostering and supervising the implementation of protection against gender-based violence and children.
2. In order to carry out the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1), the regional government establishes programs and action activities for the protection of gender and children in 1 (one) Regional Action Plan as a basis for SKPD in implementing protection against gender-based violence and children.
3. Further provisions regarding the Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2), shall be regulated by Mayor Regulation.

Article 14

1. The Regional Government conducts empowerment and awareness to families, parents, and the community by providing information, guidance and/or counseling to prevent the occurrence of criminal acts of gender-based violence and children.
2. In addition to empowerment and awareness as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government conducts efforts as follows:
a. increasing the number and quality of education both formal and non-formal and informal;
b. opening accessibility to obtain education, training, funding, income enhancement and social services;
c. opening employment opportunities;
d. building community participation and awareness to implement prevention and protection against gender-based violence and children;
e. building and providing a complete and easily accessible information system;
f. building network cooperation with law enforcement officers, government apparatus, universities and various non-governmental organizations that work and/or care about gender and children; and
g. opening complaint posts for protection against gender-based violence and children.

Article 15

1. Efforts to prevent the occurrence of gender-based violence and children as referred to in Article 14, shall be implemented by SKPD whose duties and functions are in the field of:
a. social affairs;
b. health;
c. education;
d. manpower;
e. women's empowerment and child protection;
f. public order and security; and
g. planning.
2. Prevention of acts of violence by SKPD as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be implemented in an integrated and sustainable manner based on the Regional Action Plan.

Article 16

Forms of services and assistance for victims of gender-based violence and children organized by the Women, Children and Youth Protection Institution, include:
a. medical;
b. medicolegal;
c. psychosocial;
d. legal;
e. economic independence

Article 17

The provision of services and assistance to victims of gender-based violence and children shall be conducted with the principles of:
a. fast, safe, and empathetic;
b. guarantee of confidentiality;
c. easily accessible; and
d. free of charge.

Article 18

1. Forms of services shall be implemented in accordance with minimum service standards established by the government and implemented by SKPD whose duties and functions are in the fields of:
a. social affairs;
b. health;
c. gender-based empowerment and child protection;
d. welfare;
e. education;
f. population;
g. labor.
2. In carrying out the duties and functions as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government shall cooperate with Government agencies, Provincial Government, other Regency/City Governments, and the community.

Article 20

1. Forms of Reunification provided to victims of gender-based violence and children are as follows:
a. repatriation;
b. social reintegration.
2. Repatriation services as referred to in paragraph (1) letter a include:
a. coordinating with relevant agencies;
b. implementing the victim repatriation process; and
c. providing protection to the victim's destination.
3. Social reintegration services as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b include:
a. assistance in the process of reunification with the victim's family;
b. providing information to the local community for the victim's reunification with their environment;
c. providing assurance that the family and community are ready to accept the victim back; and
d. implementing economic empowerment.

Article 21

1. The Regional Government is obligated to empower victims of violence.
2. Empowerment of victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented by the Women, Children and Youth Protection Agency.
3. In empowering victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government shall prepare empowerment programs in the economic field, which include:
a. providing the necessary requirements for entrepreneurship training for victims of violence, in order to increase business knowledge, attitudes, and skills;
b. facilitating the implementation of various job training and skills training;
c. providing assistance in developing productive economic enterprises;
d. exploring cooperation with small, medium and large enterprises, as well as financial institutions to develop businesses for victims of violence;
e. facilitating the provision of capital for victims of violence; and
f. expanding access to information and promoting the products of victims of violence.
4. In empowering victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government may establish cooperation with job training institutions organized by the private sector.

Article 24

1. In order to achieve the objectives of implementing protection against gender-based violence and children as referred to in Article 3, the Regional Government shall cooperate with:
a. the Government;
b. the Provincial Government;
c. other regency/city governments; and
d. the community.
2. Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:
a. exchange of data and information;
b. rehabilitation of victims of violence;
c. repatriation and social reintegration; and
d. provision of evidence and witnesses, and followed up in accordance with applicable law.
3. Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) shall be implemented in accordance with laws and regulations.

CHAPTER IX

COMMUNITY PARTICIPATION

Article 28

1. The community may participate in efforts to implement protection against gender-based violence and children.
2. Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) may be conducted by individuals or organizations.
3. Community participation in the implementation of protection for Victims of Gender-Based Violence and Children may be conducted through:
a. providing protection for victims of gender-based violence and children;
b. providing emergency assistance;
c. providing advocacy to victims and/or the community regarding the handling of cases of gender-based violence and children;
d. assisting in the process of submitting applications for protection orders;
e. conveying information to the competent authorities related to cases of gender-based violence and children;
f. fostering local wisdom with the principles of non-discrimination and the best interests of victims in handling gender-based violence and children;
g. organizing strengthening of community groups in handling gender-based violence and children;
h. disseminating information about laws and regulations related to gender-based violence and children.
